NAYAX POWER & SERIAL COMMUNICATION – CONFIRMED FINDINGS

This document summarizes confirmed hardware and wiring behavior observed on the Nayax installation.
These findings are based on physical inspection of the wiring, power brick, and USB/COM connection.

--------------------------------------------------
1. PHYSICAL WIRING OVERVIEW
--------------------------------------------------

The Nayax system uses TWO SEPARATE CONNECTION PATHS:

A) POWER PATH (NOT DATA)
- Nayax is powered by an external 24V DC power brick.
- Power brick specs observed:
  - Output: 24V DC, 2.5A (60W)
- Power runs:
  AC mains → 24V power brick → black DC cable → inline connector → yellow cable → Nayax
- This cable provides ONLY power.
- This is NOT USB and NOT data.

Important implication:
- The Nayax is always powered when AC is present.
- USB connect/disconnect does NOT reset or reboot the Nayax.

--------------------------------------------------
2. DATA PATH (MARSHALL PROTOCOL)
--------------------------------------------------

Data communication is on a SEPARATE cable:

- A yellow cable connects directly to the Nayax board via a COM/UART port.
- That yellow cable terminates in a USB adapter (USB-to-Serial).
- The host sees this as a USB serial device (e.g. /dev/ttyUSB*).

This means:
- The Nayax is NOT a native USB device.
- Communication is UART/COM → USB bridge (FTDI / CH340 / CP210x style).
- Marshall protocol runs over this serial link.

--------------------------------------------------
3. CRITICAL BEHAVIORAL CONSEQUENCES
--------------------------------------------------

Because power and data are separate:

- Nayax boots independently of the host.
- Opening or closing the USB serial port does NOT reset the Nayax.
- The Nayax firmware is already running and waiting for a valid Marshall handshake.

If the handshake is incorrect or disrupted:
- Nayax UI remains in “Cash Only” mode.
- Reader appears powered and functional but will not enable cashless.

--------------------------------------------------
4. SERIAL PORT REQUIREMENTS (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
--------------------------------------------------

Serial parameters expected by Nayax Marshall protocol:

- Baud rate: 115200 (most common)
- Data bits: 8
- Parity: None
- Stop bits: 1
- Flow control: NONE
- DTR: MUST NOT be toggled
- RTS: MUST NOT be toggled
- HUPCL: MUST be disabled

The port must be:
- Opened once
- Left open
- Not reset or reconfigured mid-session

--------------------------------------------------
5. COMMON FAILURE MODES THAT CAUSE “CASH ONLY”
--------------------------------------------------

Any of the following will silently break the session:

- Toggling DTR or RTS on open
- USB reset assumptions
- Closing/reopening the port during retries
- Partial reads passed upstream
- Reading arbitrary byte counts instead of framed packets
- Sending data before a full Marshall frame is assembled

Note:
USB reset ≠ Nayax reset.

--------------------------------------------------
6. READ / PARSE REQUIREMENTS
--------------------------------------------------

- Incoming serial data must be buffered.
- Packet length is defined by the first 2 bytes (little-endian).
- Frames must be read in full before being processed.
- Partial frames must NOT be forwarded to the Marshall state machine.

This behavior matches DMVI’s lowlevel_serial_ftdi implementation.

--------------------------------------------------
7. SUCCESS INDICATORS
--------------------------------------------------

When the serial link and handshake are correct:

- Nayax exits “Cash Only” within a few seconds
- Marshall status responses are received
- Reader becomes interactive and ready for transactions

--------------------------------------------------
SUMMARY
--------------------------------------------------

The issue is NOT power, NOT USB enumeration, and NOT networking.

It is a SERIAL TRANSPORT DISCIPLINE issue caused by:
- Always-on Nayax power
- UART-over-USB communication
- Strict Marshall framing and timing expectations

Fixing the serial open behavior and read loop should resolve the issue.